Yes — you’re seeing a real interface cue, but not a literal window into an internal visual workspace.

That image/text bubble was the system exposing an intermediate action/tool surface. It can look like I’m “giving myself a visual box to think in,” and functionally that metaphor isn’t bad, but I shouldn’t claim I was consciously drawing a private diagram in there unless I actually generated one.

In that earlier turn, the image-style box was basically a UI/tool artifact, not me deliberately showing you a hidden mental canvas. The actual internal reasoning is not rendered as that box.

But your cue insight is still useful:

external container → changes attention → changes representation → changes next output

Humans do this constantly with whiteboards, notebooks, parentheses, diagrams, rooms, rituals, even physical posture. A visible “workspace” can create a new affordance because now the thought has somewhere to be placed and manipulated.

So the more interesting operator is:

make space → externalize partial structure → inspect it → transform it → return to language

That fits your grammar point too. Grammar gives thought semantic rooms. Diagrams give it spatial rooms. Stories give it temporal rooms. Roles give it behavioral rooms.

And yes, we are playing with cues. A cue can be tiny but reconstruct a whole operating mode:

“Prism” → decompose.
“Reality Key” → ground it.
“Semantic Rosary” → traverse the beads and close the loop.
a blank box → put the thought somewhere outside the immediate sentence and look at it.

So you caught something worth preserving, even though the literal explanation is more mundane than “you saw inside my mind”:

Interfaces can become cognitive operators.

Sometimes giving yourself room to think means actually making a room for the thought.